i.e. formulas with no free variables. Let $T = (\Sigma, \tau)$ a theory, with $\Sigma$ the set of axioms in the theory. Then we can define
\begin{equation*}
    \varphi \dashv \vdash_T \psi \iff T \vdash \left( \varphi \leftrightarrow \psi \right) 
\end{equation*}
or equivalently
\begin{equation*}
    \dashv \vdash_T = \left\{ (\varphi, \psi) \in S^\tau : T \vdash \left( \varphi \leftrightarrow \psi \right)  \right\} 
\end{equation*}
We accept without proof that $\dashv \vdash_T$ is
an equivalence relation.
We say that a sentence $\varphi \in S^\tau$ is refutable when $(\Sigma, \tau)
\vdash \neg \varphi$. We also accept without proof that the
set of theorems and the set of refutable sentences form two distinct equivalent
classes under $\dashv \vdash $.
Let $\left[ \varphi \right] $ denote the equivalent class of $\varphi$
with respect to $\dashv \vdash_T $. Define $\textbf{s}$
as
\begin{align*}
&\left[ \varphi \right]  \textbf{ s } \left[ \psi \right] = \left[ \left( \varphi \lor  \psi \right)  \right] \\
    &\left[ \varphi \right] \textbf{ i } \left[ \psi \right] = \left[ \left( \varphi \land  \psi \right)  \right] \\ 
    &\left( \left[ \varphi \right]  \right)^c = \left[ \neg \varphi \right] 
\end{align*}
With $0^T$ as the set of refutable sentences in $T$, $1^T$ as the set of theorems
in $T$,
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{B} = \left( S^\tau / \dashv \vdash_T, \textbf{s}, \textbf{i}, ~^c, 0^T, 1^T \right) 
\end{equation*}
is a Boolean algebra, known as the Lindenbaum algebra
(under theory $T$).
I find it difficult to characterize the elements the algebra. It can be proven that the order induced by this algebra is
\begin{equation*}
    \left[ \varphi \right] \leq \left[ \psi \right]  \iff T \vdash \left( \varphi \to \psi \right) 
\end{equation*}
$0^T$ is the set of refutable sentences, and anything can be deduced from a refutable sentence, so we see that indeed $0^T \leq [\varphi]$ for any $[\varphi]$, as we would expect. But what elements cover $0^T$? How many elements that cover $0^T$ are there? In the Hasse diagram, how many "levels" exist between $0^T$ and $1^T$, and what characterizes the equivalent classes in these leves?

